  "problem": "The $13$th root of unity ${e}^{22\\pi \\text{i}/13}$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{e}^{22\\pi \\text{i}/13}={z}^{p},\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ is the principal $13$th root of unity. What is the value of $p?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that the $13$th roots of unity can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}/13}\n$$\n\nwhere $k=0,1,\u2026,12.$ \n\nWe obtain the *principal* $13$th root of unity by substituting $k=1:$\n\n$$\nz={e}^{2\\cdot 1\\cdot \\pi \\text{i}/13}={e}^{2\\pi \\text{i}/13}\n$$\n\nWe can write the given root of unity as a power of the principal root as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{22\\pi \\text{i}/13} & =({e}^{2\\pi \\text{i}/13})^{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=11.$",
